# {{ workspace_name }}

This directory has its own **separate** Pi devcontainer; the parent repository
uses its original `.devcontainer/` configuration unchanged. Open **this folder**
(`/path/to/repo/{{ workspace_name }}`), not the parent repository, in a second
Visual Studio Code window. Choose **Dev Containers: Reopen in Container**.

## Filesystem contract

| In Pi container | Access | Host content |
|---|---|---|
| `/project` | read-only | parent repository, including this folder |
| `/workspace/{{ workspace_name }}` | read-only | this folder (templates, extension, runner) |
| `/results` | read/write | `{{ workspace_name }}/results` |
| `/home/node/.pi` | read/write | persistent named volume for agent state |

**Important:** The results directory is physically inside the parent repo on
the host and writable through `/results`, even though `/project` is read-only.
Do not place source code inside `results/` and treat experiment outputs as
untrusted. No container is a security boundary against its host's kernel or
against a privileged Docker socket; no Docker socket is mounted. Only root
startup receives the capabilities needed to install the firewall and drop to
`node`; Pi and experiments run without capabilities or sudo.

## First run

1. Open this folder in its own devcontainer window and build the container.
2. In the new container, verify the mounts:

   ```bash
   test -r /project && test -w /results
   if touch /project/.must-not-write 2>/dev/null; then echo 'UNSAFE: project writable'; exit 1; fi
   if touch /workspace/{{ workspace_name }}/.must-not-write 2>/dev/null; then echo 'UNSAFE: experiment setup writable'; exit 1; fi
   ```

3. Run a smoke test without a model:

   ```bash
   mkdir -p /results/manual-smoke
   bash experiments/run.sh /results/manual-smoke 'python3 /workspace/{{ workspace_name }}/experiments/benchmark.py --project /project --output "$RUN_DIR" --seed 42'
   cat /results/manual-smoke/metrics.json
   ```

4. Configure your Pi model/provider using `pi` authentication or environment
   variables, and then launch the restricted agent:

   ```bash
   ./run-pi.sh
   ```

5. Tell Pi which experiment to use: “Use `/project/experiment.py`; read its
   `--help` and run a small experiment.” Pi can inspect source and execute shell
   commands through `run_experiment` without changing the runner.

   For example, discovery uses:

   ```json
   {"command": "python3 /project/experiment.py --help"}
   ```

   Each call starts in a fresh `/results/<uuid>` directory. Use absolute project
   paths and `$RUN_DIR` for outputs; `cd /project` explicitly if required by a
   script. The command, logs, and exit status are saved. Metrics are optional;
   a JSON object at `$RUN_DIR/metrics.json` enables numeric metric ranking.

## Iteration

Edit project experiments from your **regular development devcontainer** and
name them in chat; no prescribed filename or CLI is required. This experiment
container intentionally cannot edit source or its setup. Rebuild the container
when you change its Dockerfile. If changing extension code, use `/reload` in Pi.

For project dependencies, create a runtime environment in `/results/.venv`,
not `/project/.venv`. `UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT` and `UV_CACHE_DIR` are already
set under `/results`. To match complex scientific dependencies, modify the Pi
Dockerfile or base it on the same runtime image as your development container.
GPU access is not enabled; configure it deliberately if required.

## Outbound firewall

The firewall applies to Pi and every experiment command by default. Root startup
installs fail-closed IPv4/IPv6 filter rules before resolving allowlisted hosts,
then uses `setpriv` to run the command as `node` with all capability bounding
bits dropped. Docker drops all capabilities except startup-only `NET_ADMIN`,
`SETUID`, `SETGID`, and `SETPCAP`; no-new-privileges and the PID limit remain.
The agent cannot modify the root-owned firewall or obtain firewall privileges.
Configuration or DNS resolution failure stops startup with restrictive rules
remaining in place.

Allowed traffic is loopback, inbound responses to permitted connections, DNS
(TCP/UDP port 53) to IPv4 nameservers from `/etc/resolv.conf`, and IPv4 TCP
port 443 to A records resolved at startup for allowlisted hosts. Other outbound
traffic is rejected, including external IPv6. Docker NAT is preserved. There
are no SSH, host-gateway, broad GitHub CIDR, or blanket telemetry exceptions.

The defaults cover Pi/model/auth (`api.anthropic.com`, `api.openai.com`,
`auth.openai.com`, `chatgpt.com`, `pi.dev`), package downloads
(`registry.npmjs.org`, `pypi.org`, `files.pythonhosted.org`), GitHub
(`github.com`, `api.github.com`, `raw.githubusercontent.com`,
`objects.githubusercontent.com`), and editor services
(`marketplace.visualstudio.com`, `vscode.blob.core.windows.net`,
`update.code.visualstudio.com`).

For a different provider or dataset host, edit
`.devcontainer/firewall-allowlist.txt` **on the host or in your regular
development devcontainer**, then rebuild this Pi container. Use one hostname
per line; comments and blank lines are accepted. The Dockerfile copies this
allowlist, `.devcontainer/init-firewall.py`, and `.devcontainer/entrypoint.sh`
into root-owned `/usr/local/lib/autoresearch/`. Changing a mounted copy does
not alter the active policy; the agent cannot edit the baked files.

This is an IP allowlist, not domain-level security or total network isolation.
Shared IPs may expose other services; permitted services and DNS can be used
for data exfiltration. External access is IPv4 HTTPS only. DNS address rotation
requires restarting the container to resolve again. Image builds are not
filtered by the runtime firewall. Commands still can read credential files.

Manual checks inside this container, using `curl` if available:

```bash
curl --noproxy '*' --connect-timeout 10 -I https://api.openai.com
curl --noproxy '*' --connect-timeout 10 -I https://example.com
curl --noproxy '*' --connect-timeout 10 -6 -I 'https://[2606:4700:4700::1111]'
```

The first should return an HTTP status (any status proves reachability; do not
use `-f`). The other two should fail to connect. These checks do not establish
domain security or prevent exfiltration through allowed destinations.

## Trust and limitations

- `run_experiment` provides general shell execution with the container user's
  permissions and the same outbound firewall as Pi. The tool allowlist is not
  a shell sandbox.
- The extension passes a minimal environment rather than model credentials,
  but commands can still read accessible credential files, including Pi state.
- The read-only project mount prevents filesystem edits through that mount,
  but does not redact potentially sensitive source files from Pi.
- The image uses a conventional writable container layer for system temporary
  files. Only `/results` is writable back to the project host.
- Generated file updates are intentional: regenerate with `--force` only after
  reviewing changes; this replaces generated files.

Reference: https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/main/packages/coding-agent/docs/extensions.md
